Depends: ${misc:Depends}, ${shlibs:Depends}, nodejs (>= 18), python3, python3-jsonschema, plugin-hostd (>= 0.20.5~), plugin-hostd (<< 0.21~), omx-clap-host (>= 0.32.3~), omx-clap-host (<< 0.33~), libomx-clap-core1 (>= 0.32.4~), libomx-clap-core1 (<< 0.33~), liblilv-0-0, ${lilv:Depends}
# mod-host is the comparison host: --host-profile mod-host and the hosting sweep run it when it is installed, and
# without it those verdicts read mod-host.not-installed. Nothing else uses it, so it is only suggested; a machine
# that runs a published comparison or benchmark installs mod-host (and jalv) itself.
Suggests: mod-host
Description: find out whether an audio plugin is safe to run live
 A plugin that glitches on stage is worse than one you never installed.
 plugin-qualify takes an LV2 or CLAP plugin and tells you whether it can be
 trusted inside a real-time audio engine: it scans the plugin, measures its
 latency and CPU cost at the sample rates and buffer sizes you play at, runs
 it under a real host with memory checking, and gives one plain verdict.
 Anything it could not measure is never a pass.
 .
 Use it to vet a plugin before a gig, to curate a plugin collection, or to
 judge the same plugin against the rules of different hosts.
 .
 Its documentation is on openmixer.org.

